<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>text_6_odd_even</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Type each function as an expression in \(x\).</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><div>Give an example of an odd function. \(f_1(x)=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:odd]]</div></li>
<li><div>Give an example of an even function. \(f_2(x)=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:even]]</div></li>
<li><div>Give an example of a function which is both odd and even. \(f_3(x)=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:oddeven]]</div></li>
<li><div>Is the answer to part 3 unique, or are there many different possibilities? Unique: [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:unique]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A function is <strong>even</strong> if \(f(-x) = f(x)\) for every \(x\), and <strong>odd</strong> if \(f(-x) = -f(x)\) for every \(x\). This question tests these properties of the expression you give, so many different answers are correct. For an odd function we check that \[ f(x)+f(-x)=0,\] and for an even function that \[ f(x)-f(-x)=0.\]</p>
<p>Powers give the simplest examples: \(x^3\), \(x\) and \(x^5 - 2x\) are odd, while \(x^4\), \(x^2 + 1\) and any constant are even. On a graph, an even function is symmetric about the \(y\)-axis, and an odd function is unchanged by a half-turn (a rotation through \(180^\circ\)) about the origin.</p>
<p>A function that is both odd and even satisfies \(f(x) = f(-x) = -f(x)\), so \(2f(x) = 0\) and \(f(x) = 0\) for every \(x\). The zero function is the only example, so the answer to part 3 is unique.</p>
<p>Drag the points \(P\) and \(Q\) below. \(P'\) is the mirror image of \(P\) in the \(y\)-axis and always stays on the even curve. \(Q'\) is the image of \(Q\) under a half-turn about the origin and always stays on the odd curve.</p>
<div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="Interactive graph of an even curve y equals x squared over 2 and an odd curve y equals x cubed over 4. A draggable point P on the even curve has its mirror image P prime in the y-axis; a draggable point Q on the odd curve has its image Q prime under a half-turn about the origin.">
[[jsxgraph width="360px" height="300px"]]
(function() {
  var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [-4, 5, 4, -5],
    axis: true, grid: false, showNavigation: false, showCopyright: false
  });
  var evenCurve = board.create('functiongraph', [function(x){ return 0.5*x*x; }, -4, 4],
    {strokeColor: '#1565c0', strokeWidth: 2, fixed: true, highlight: false});
  var oddCurve = board.create('functiongraph', [function(x){ return 0.25*x*x*x; }, -4, 4],
    {strokeColor: '#2e7d32', strokeWidth: 2, fixed: true, highlight: false});
  var P = board.create('glider', [-1.2, 0.72, evenCurve], {name: 'P', color: '#1565c0', size: 4, label: {offset: [8, 10]}});
  var Pm = board.create('point', [function(){ return -P.X(); }, function(){ return P.Y(); }],
    {name: "P'", color: '#1565c0', size: 3, fixed: true, label: {offset: [-22, 10]}});
  var Q = board.create('glider', [2.2, 2.662, oddCurve], {name: 'Q', color: '#2e7d32', size: 4, label: {offset: [10, -10]}});
  var Qm = board.create('point', [function(){ return -Q.X(); }, function(){ return -Q.Y(); }],
    {name: "Q'", color: '#2e7d32', size: 3, fixed: true, label: {offset: [-24, 10]}});
  board.create('segment', [P, Pm], {strokeColor: '#1565c0', strokeWidth: 1, dash: 2, fixed: true});
  board.create('segment', [Q, Qm], {strokeColor: '#2e7d32', strokeWidth: 1, dash: 2, fixed: true});
  board.create('text', [0.4, -3.9, 'Even: y = x²/2 (P, P′)'], {fixed: true, color: '#1565c0'});
  board.create('text', [0.4, -4.5, 'Odd: y = x³/4 (Q, Q′)'], {fixed: true, color: '#2e7d32'});
})();
